## Bug 1: forward --no-meta, --filter-prog, --fs-events from `mise watch` to watchexec

Prototype patch against `https://github.com/jdx/mise.git` HEAD `70c2f0ba06bca99417d3f0e416ef0363ec91bf2e`
(`v2026.5.11-1-g70c2f0b`). Applies cleanly with `git apply` from the mise repo root.

```diff
diff --git a/src/cli/watch.rs b/src/cli/watch.rs
index f66a665..a87a01b 100644
--- a/src/cli/watch.rs
+++ b/src/cli/watch.rs
@@ -176,6 +176,39 @@ impl Watch {
                 args.push(pattern.to_string());
             }
         }
+        if self.watchexec.filter_fs_meta {
+            args.push("--no-meta".to_string());
+        }
+        // Forward --fs-events only when the user picked a non-default set.
+        // The clap default (create,remove,rename,modify,metadata) matches
+        // watchexec's own default, so an unset --fs-events is identical to
+        // omitting the flag. Forwarding the default unconditionally would
+        // also conflict with watchexec's `--no-meta` because both ship
+        // event-filter state.
+        if !self.watchexec.filter_fs_events.is_empty()
+            && self.watchexec.filter_fs_events.as_slice() != DEFAULT_FS_EVENTS
+        {
+            args.push("--fs-events".to_string());
+            args.push(
+                self.watchexec
+                    .filter_fs_events
+                    .iter()
+                    .map(|e| {
+                        e.to_possible_value()
+                            .expect("FsEvent variants all have ValueEnum names")
+                            .get_name()
+                            .to_string()
+                    })
+                    .collect::<Vec<_>>()
+                    .join(","),
+            );
+        }
+        if !self.watchexec.filter_programs.is_empty() {
+            for prog in &self.watchexec.filter_programs {
+                args.push("--filter-prog".to_string());
+                args.push(prog.clone());
+            }
+        }
         if let Some(watch_file) = &self.watchexec.watch_file {
             args.push("--watch-file".to_string());
             args.push(watch_file.to_string_lossy().to_string());
@@ -1238,6 +1271,17 @@ pub enum FsEvent {
     Metadata,
 }

+/// Mirror of the `--fs-events` clap default. Kept in sync with the
+/// `default_value` on `WatchexecArgs::filter_fs_events` so the forward path
+/// can recognise an unchanged value and omit the flag.
+const DEFAULT_FS_EVENTS: &[FsEvent] = &[
+    FsEvent::Create,
+    FsEvent::Remove,
+    FsEvent::Rename,
+    FsEvent::Modify,
+    FsEvent::Metadata,
+];
+
 #[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, PartialEq, ValueEnum)]
 #[clap(rename_all = "lower")]
 pub enum ShellCompletion {
```

Mise advertises `-J` as its CLI-facing alias because lowercase `-j` is mise's own `--jobs`
flag. The patch emits the long form `--filter-prog` to the subprocess (unambiguous and
accepted by watchexec), so no `-J` to `-j` translation is needed.

Forwarding is gated for `--fs-events`: the clap default
`create,remove,rename,modify,metadata` matches watchexec's own default, so passing it
unconditionally would add redundant noise (and would collide with `--no-meta` when both
defaults are populated by clap).

`cargo test --release -p mise` (filtered to `cli::watch`): 4/4 pass post-patch
(`merge_dedupes_across_tasks`, `merge_does_not_let_one_task_exclude_anothers_include`,
`merge_single_task_splits_pos_and_neg`, `merge_unescapes_literal_bang`).
Full mise binary unit suite (`cargo test --release -p mise --bins`): 956/956 pass.

## Bug 5: break the Arc cycle keeping FilterProgs alive past shutdown

Prototype patch against `https://github.com/watchexec/watchexec.git` HEAD
`9d8e3443ee5fbbf07baa0e0bff2c2c63d40f1a4f` (the doc's cited reference; both
files cited are byte-identical to the 2.5.1 release tag). Applies cleanly with
`git apply` from the watchexec repo root.

```diff
diff --git a/crates/cli/src/config.rs b/crates/cli/src/config.rs
index 1765544..1f6d69c 100644
--- a/crates/cli/src/config.rs
+++ b/crates/cli/src/config.rs
@@ -98,7 +98,12 @@ pub fn make_config(args: &Args, state: &State) -> Result<Config> {
        let clear = args.output.screen_clear;

        let emit_events_to = args.events.emit_events_to;
-       let state = state.clone();
+       // Break the Arc<Config> -> action-handler-closure -> Arc<InnerState> ->
+       // OnceLock<Arc<Watchexec>> -> Arc<Config> reference cycle that otherwise
+       // keeps Config alive past runtime shutdown, pinning FilterProgs' Sender
+       // and parking its spawn_blocking thread in receiver.blocking_recv()
+       // forever (manifests as a SIGINT hang when `-j` is used).
+       let state = Arc::downgrade(state);

        if args.only_emit_events {
                config.on_action(move |mut action| {
@@ -206,7 +211,14 @@ pub fn make_config(args: &Args, state: &State) -> Result<Config> {
        config.on_action_async(move |mut action| {
                let add_envs = add_envs.clone();
                let command = command.clone();
-               let state = state.clone();
+               // `state` is `Weak<InnerState>` (see make_config above); upgrade for
+               // this invocation. The handler only runs while the runtime is live,
+               // so a `None` here means we are mid-shutdown and the safe response is
+               // to return the action untouched.
+               let Some(state) = state.upgrade() else {
+                       debug!("InnerState dropped before action; skipping handler");
+                       return Box::new(async move { action });
+               };
                let queued = queued.clone();
                let quit_again = quit_again.clone();
                let paused = paused.clone();
```

The patch breaks the strong-reference cycle by capturing `Weak<InnerState>` in
the action-handler closure and upgrading per-invocation. During normal
operation the upgrade always succeeds (the handler only runs while the runtime
is alive). At shutdown, the closure no longer pins `Arc<InnerState>`, so the
`Arc<Watchexec>` -> `Arc<Config>` -> `Arc<WatchexecFilterer>` -> `Sender`
chain drops, the `FilterProgs` blocking thread sees `None`, and the runtime
exits.

Verification (scripted SIGINT-hang reproducer at `repro/sigint-check.sh`
inside the prototype workspace; sends SIGINT, polls for exit, SIGKILLs and
reports HANG if the process is still alive after 6s):

```text
[prepatch/inline] HANG: alive 5.67s after SIGINT; killing with SIGKILL
[prepatch/file]   HANG: alive 5.87s after SIGINT; killing with SIGKILL
[postpatch/inline] OK: exited within 0.21s after SIGINT (rc=0)
[postpatch/file]   OK: exited within 0.00s after SIGINT (rc=0)
```

Control runs (same invocation without `-j`) exit in 0.00s both pre- and
post-patch, confirming the bug is specific to the `-j` code path and the
patch does not regress the non-`-j` shutdown.

`cargo test --workspace --release` post-patch: all suites pass
(`watchexec_signals` 5/5, `watchexec_supervisor` 8/8, doc-tests across
`watchexec_cli`, `watchexec_events`, `watchexec_filterer_globset`,
`watchexec_filterer_ignore` clean; full log saved at
`/tmp/watchexec-fix-SoZlgn/cargo-test.log` during the prototype session).
